  "answer": "The cleansing action of soap is due to the unique structure of soap molecules. Each soap molecule has two ends: one is hydrophilic (water-loving) and the other is hydrophobic (water-repelling). When soap is mixed with water, the hydrophobic end of the soap molecule attaches to dirt or grease on the cloth, while the hydrophilic end remains in contact with water. This causes the soap molecules to form structures called micelles, where the hydrophobic ends cluster together around the dirt particles, and the hydrophilic ends face outward, allowing the dirt to be suspended in water. As a result, the dirt is dispersed in water and can be rinsed away, leaving the cloth clean.",
